  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d1896a70",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#  An overview of sampling modes : Metropolis, MALA, HMC and Langevin   \n",
    "\n",
    "This notebook uses the [pyALF](https://github.com/ALF-QMC/pyALF) interface to solve the 6-site Hubbard chain with different sampling schemes included in the ALF code. Aside from the Langevin mode — where the discretized-time evolution introduces a systematic error — all schemes sample the same underlying distribution and should reproduce the same energy within error bars. The notebook\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Builds ALF from the master branch.\n",
    "2. Sets up the parameter file for the 6-site Hubbard model.\n",
    "3. Compiles and loops over various updating schemes.\n",
    "4. At the end, a comparison of energies is provided.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Overview of updating schemes\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Sequential Metropolis (discrete field)**: The classic single-spin-flip Metropolis update. The Hubbard-Stratonovich field is discrete (Ising-like), and each field variable is proposed to flip and accepted/rejected via the Metropolis ratio.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Sequential Metropolis (continuous field)**: Same single-site sweep structure, but the Hubbard-Stratonovich field is continuous. Proposals are drawn from a symmetric window of width `Amplitude` around the current value.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Sequential MALA (Metropolis-Adjusted Langevin Algorithm)**: A continuous-field, single-site update where the proposal is drawn using the local force (gradient of the action) as a drift term, followed by a Metropolis accept/reject step to guarantee detailed balance. Controlled by `Delta_t_MALA_sequential` (step size) and `Max_Force_MALA_sequential` (force clipping for stability).\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Global tau moves**: Instead of updating one field variable at a time, all fields, or a user defined set of fields are updated together on the time slice.  This can be combined with plain Metropolis or MALA-type proposals (`Delta_t_MALA_global_tau`, `Max_Force_MALA_global_tau`) to improve autocorrelation/ergodicity, especially in `Continuous` field mode.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Global moves**: Updates involving the whole field configuration (all time slices and sites) proposed simultaneously, again optionally combined with a MALA-like drift (`Global_MALA_moves`, `Delta_t_MALA_global`, `Max_Force_MALA_global`) and repeated for `N_Global`/`N_Global_MALA_sweeps` sub-sweeps per global step.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Langevin dynamics**: A continuous-field update that evolves the fields using a stochastic differential equation (deterministic drift from the force plus Gaussian noise), with discretization time step `Delta_t_Langevin_HMC`. There is *no* Metropolis correction step, so this introduces a systematic bias of order `Delta_t_Langevin_HMC` that vanishes only in the limit of small time step.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Hybrid Monte Carlo (HMC)**: Introduces fictitious momenta conjugate to the continuous fields and integrates Hamilton's equations via a leapfrog scheme (`Leapfrog_steps` steps of size `Delta_t_Langevin_HMC`), followed by a Metropolis accept/reject step on the full trajectory. This removes the systematic Langevin bias while still exploiting gradient information for efficient, low-autocorrelation moves.\n",
    "\n",
    "See the [Documentation](https://alf.physik.uni-wuerzburg.de/doc.pdf) in the ALF source tree for the full technical description of the update algorithms and force computations."
   ]
  },
  {
